        <div class="section-title">Page 2: Laryngeal Histology &amp; Tracheal Wall Layers</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">9. Which lining pattern covers the lingual surface and the apical margin of the Epiglottis?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Simple cuboidal non-ciliated epithelium</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Stratified columnar with highly developed microvilli borders</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">10. Which combination lists only laryngeal cartilages structurally classified under the Elastic variety?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Thyroid, Cricoid, and lower base of the Arytenoid</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Cricoid, Trachealis framework, and major Bronchial plates</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Epiglottis, Corniculate, Cuneiform, and tips of the Arytenoid</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Thyroid, Epiglottis, and complete vocal rings</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">11. Which major laryngeal cartilages are composed of Hyaline cartilage, predisposing them to calcification later in life?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Epiglottis and Cuneiform structures</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Thyroid, Cricoid, and most of the Arytenoid cartilage</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Corniculate and vocal process tips exclusively</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> None; all laryngeal supportive structures are strictly fibrocartilaginous</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">12. Which elements are localized within the True Vocal Cords (Vocal Folds) to support phonation?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Seromucous laryngeal glands and respiratory epithelium covering</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Smooth muscle fibers forming the circular sphincter lines</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Hyaline plates embedded directly within loose mucosal reflections</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Vocal ligament (parallel elastic fibers), Vocalis muscle (skeletal), and stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">13. The mucosal layer of the human Trachea is structurally composed of which components?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Respiratory epithelium and a lamina propria rich in elastic fibers and diffuse lymphocytes</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Stratified squamous epithelium and dense inner longitudinal skeletal layers</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Simple squamous lining resting directly over deep sub-mucosal serous glands</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Clara-cell dominated lining sheet and sub-adjacent hyaline plates</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">14. What structural feature characterizes the Submucosa layer of the human Trachea?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Complete absence of blood channels and absolute replacement by avascular elastic networks</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Clusters of large unmyelinated neuronal cell bodies acting as local sensory filters</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Presence of mixed seromucous glands producing part of the airway fluid sheen</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Concentric rings of dense smooth muscle completely unattached to outer components</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">15. How many hyaline cartilage rings are typically found in the tracheal adventitia, and what is their configuration?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> 50-60 complete closed concentric osseous rings</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> 10-12 C-shaped hyaline cartilage rings open posteriorly</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> 5-7 irregular triangular elastic plates overlapping vertically</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Exactly 20 spiral-shaped fibrocartilage loops wrapping downwards</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">16. What is the tissue composition and function of the Trachealis muscle?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Striated skeletal muscle that prevents collapsing during forced vocalization</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Elastic connective tissue bands that permanently anchor the esophagus</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Cardiac-like syncytial fibers generating regular contractility waves</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Smooth muscle bridging the open posterior gap of C-shaped tracheal cartilages to adjust lumen size</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">
                ANSWER KEY 📍 Q9: A | Q10: C | Q11: B | Q12: D | Q13: A | Q14: C | Q15: B | Q16: D
            </div>
            <div class="footer-meta">
                <div>Suez University • Faculty of Medicine</div>
