        <div class="section-title">Page 3: Respiratory Epithelial Diversity &amp; Bronchial Changes</div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">17. Which cell type represents the most numerous population within the classical respiratory epithelium?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Ciliated columnar cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Goblet cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Basal stem cells</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Kulchitsky neuroendocrine cells</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">18. Which histochemical stain and light microscopic appearance uniquely identifies functional Goblet cells?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Strongly Sudan Black positive with dense lipid droplets filling the base</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Deeply eosinophilic cytoplasm containing large central crystalline rods</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> PAS-positive reaction with a pale, foamy apical cytoplasm containing mucinogen granules</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Silver impregnation positive revealing elongated cytoplasmic pseudopods</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">19. What is the functional importance of the short Basal cells resting exclusively on the tracheal basement membrane?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Phagocytosing macro-particulate impurities that bypass mechanical hairs</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Serving as reserve stem cells capable of dividing to regenerate other epithelial cell types</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Directly secreting watery fluids into the sub-mucosal gland systems</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Propelling mucus sheets upward via coordination with nerve networks</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">20. Which structural feature and role defines Brush Cells within the respiratory mucosal lining?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Abundant motile cilia that beat downward toward the pharyngeal exit</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Massive accumulations of lamellar bodies secreting specialized localized mucus splits</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Complete absence of nucleated bodies, functioning as transient metabolic bridges</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Elongated microvilli on the apical surface with sensory nerve endings synapsing at the base</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">21. To which integrated regulatory network do the Small Granule Cells (Kulchitsky cells) belong?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>A)</strong> Diffuse Neuroendocrine System (DNES / APUD)</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Reticuloendothelial macrophage clearing system</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Elastic monitoring mechanoreceptor network</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Exocrine fluid transport system</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">22. As the respiratory tree branches from primary bronchi down to intrapulmonary bronchi, how do structural elements change?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Cartilage rings become thicker, while smooth muscle layers completely vanish</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Lining epithelium thickens into stratified squamous sheets containing more glands</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>C)</strong> Cartilage rings are replaced by isolated plates, smooth muscle relative thickness increases, and glands decrease</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Alveolar outpocketings dominate the upper mucosal margins, obliterating the muscularis layer</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">23. Which structures are completely and characteristically absent from the wall of a Bronchiole?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Smooth muscle bundles and elastic fiber sheets</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>B)</strong> Cartilage plates and sub-mucosal glands</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Clara cells and tight junction networks</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>D)</strong> Microvascular capillary loops and structural collagen strands</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="question-box">
            <div class="question-text">24. What is the cytological appearance of Clara cells (Club cells) under the light microscope?</div>
            <div class="options-container">
                <div class="option-item"><strong>A)</strong> Highly flattened squamous shapes bearing long motile flagella extensions</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>B)</strong> Low cuboidal profiles containing massive peripheral vacuoles full of basic mucus</div>
                <div class="option-item"><strong>C)</strong> Multicellular clusters containing visible eccentric brown pigment grains</div>
                <div class="option-item correct-answer"><strong>D)</strong> Dome-shaped, non-ciliated cells projecting prominently into the bronchiolar lumen</div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="footer-wrapper">
            <div class="key-banner">
                ANSWER KEY 📍 Q17: A | Q18: C | Q19: B | Q20: D | Q21: A | Q22: C | Q23: B | Q24: D
            </div>
